  <div class="topbar"><div class="wrap"><div class="brandline"><span class="seed" aria-hidden="true">✳</span> Rustic Garden · Community & Digital Inclusion</div><div>Proposed event · UA Day 2027</div></div></div>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">One Internet · Many Languages · Rural Enterprise</div>
        <h1>From the Coffee Farm to the <em>Global Internet</em></h1>
        <p class="lead">A practical Universal Acceptance day built around one question: can digital systems make it easier for rural producers to reach the people and markets they choose?</p>
        <div class="meta"><span class="pill">📍 Kavutiri, Embu County, Kenya</span><span class="pill">📅 Proposed: 22 April 2027</span><span class="pill">◷ 10:00–17:00 EAT</span></div>
        <div class="actions"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="#programme">Explore the day ↓</a><button class="btn btn-ghost" type="button" onclick="window.print()">Print programme</button></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="hero-note" aria-label="Event at a glance"><div class="big">One farmer.<br>One real test.<br>Evidence that matters.</div><hr><strong>What this is</strong><p>A hands-on adoption and demonstration event—not just a talk about technology.</p><hr><strong>Who it is for</strong><p>Farmers, cooperatives, local businesses, educators, youth, public stakeholders and technology practitioners.</p></aside>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section class="section section-white" id="objective"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="kicker">The objective</div><h2>Make digital inclusion useful in real life.</h2><p>The farmer is the starting point. His coffee, knowledge, language and choices are assets—not problems to be fixed.</p></div><div class="objective"><strong>Event Objective:</strong> To demonstrate, through the experience of a smallholder coffee farmer in Kavutiri, how Universal Acceptance (UA) can remove technical barriers in digital systems used by rural enterprises. The event will test selected websites, online forms and email workflows, document compatibility gaps and corrective actions, and identify practical next steps for connecting farmers and local businesses with cooperatives, buyers and markets.</div><p class="notice"><strong>Important distinction:</strong> UA helps digital systems correctly accept and process valid domain names and email addresses, including internationalized forms. It does not by itself provide internet coverage, a smartphone, market demand or higher coffee prices. The event will measure technical results separately from commercial outcomes.</p></div></section>

    <section class="section" id="farmer"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="kicker">The human story</div><h2>Start with the farmer—not the device.</h2><p>Imagine a 50-year-old coffee grower who speaks Kiembu and Kiswahili, uses a basic feature phone, lives in a solar-powered rural home and has rarely travelled beyond his county. We begin by listening to how he already works.</p></div><div class="grid3">
      <article class="card"><div class="num">01</div><h3>Listen in his language</h3><p>Ask how he sells coffee, who determines the price, how he hears from buyers, what he trusts, and what he wants to change. Do not assume he wants an app.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><div class="num">02</div><h3>Find the actual barrier</h3><p>Check whether the problem is market access, transport, price transparency, connectivity, digital skills—or a real technical failure involving domain names or email addresses.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><div class="num">03</div><h3>Test and follow through</h3><p>With permission, test a relevant digital journey, record evidence, demonstrate a feasible correction, and identify a realistic route to a cooperative or potential buyer.</p></article>
    </div><div class="disclaimer">The farmer's story is a proposed use case. The event team will confirm the farmer's actual needs and obtain consent before using his story, farm details, product information or photographs publicly.</div></div></section>

    <section class="section section-white" id="programme"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="kicker">The day · 10:00–17:00 EAT</div><h2>From lived experience to tested evidence.</h2><p>Speakers and technical specialists will be invited; names and affiliations will be added only after participation is confirmed.</p></div><div class="agenda">
      <div class="slot"><div class="time">10:00–10:30</div><div><h3>Welcome and the farmer's journey</h3><p>A local farmer describes how coffee is grown, sold and communicated about today. A Kiembu/Kiswahili facilitator records needs and barriers.</p><span class="tag">Listen</span></div></div>
      <div class="slot"><div class="time">10:30–12:00</div><div><h3>Universal Acceptance in plain language</h3><p>A requested UA specialist explains how websites, forms and email systems should handle valid internationalized domain names and email addresses—and why this matters for participation.</p><span class="tag">Understand</span></div></div>
      <div class="slot"><div class="time">12:00–13:00</div><div><h3>Coffee, customers and opportunity</h3><p>Farmers, cooperatives and enterprises map a real market journey: product information, buyer enquiries, sample requests, communication and follow-up.</p><span class="tag">Identify use cases</span></div></div>
      <div class="slot"><div class="time">13:00–14:00</div><div><h3>Lunch and community networking</h3><p>Informal discussion across farming, education, business and technology stakeholders.</p><span class="tag">Connect</span></div></div>
      <div class="slot"><div class="time">14:00–16:00</div><div><h3>Hands-on technical adoption demonstration</h3><p>Test selected, authorized websites, forms or email workflows. Capture pass/fail evidence, reproduce relevant issues, demonstrate a feasible correction where possible, and retest.</p><span class="tag">Test · Fix · Retest</span></div></div>
      <div class="slot"><div class="time">16:00–16:40</div><div><h3>Geography, access and local business</h3><p>Map participant locations and represented stakeholder groups. Identify which organizations or services could act on the findings and what follow-up is realistic.</p><span class="tag">Measure reach</span></div></div>
      <div class="slot"><div class="time">16:40–17:00</div><div><h3>Findings and commitments</h3><p>Present test results, unresolved issues, assigned next steps and opportunities for continued collaboration.</p><span class="tag">Report</span></div></div>
    </div></div></section>

    <section class="section" id="method"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="kicker">The delivery method</div><h2>A clear chain of accountability.</h2><p>Inspired by evidence-led operational scorecards: activity alone is not success. Each step needs an expected condition, evidence, an accountable action and a result.</p></div><div class="journey">
      <div class="journey-step"><h3>Expected condition</h3><p>A selected digital system accepts and processes valid internationalized domain names and email addresses.</p></div>
      <div class="journey-step"><h3>Actual evidence</h3><p>Test records, screenshots, form outcomes, delivery results and reproducible failure details.</p></div>
      <div class="journey-step"><h3>Controlled action</h3><p>Assign an owner, demonstrate a feasible correction where possible, then retest.</p></div>
      <div class="journey-step"><h3>Verified result</h3><p>Report what passed, what failed, what changed and what remains unresolved.</p></div>
      <div class="journey-step"><h3>Follow-up</h3><p>Record responsibility, next steps and evidence needed after the event.</p></div>
    </div><p class="notice">A successful test is not proof of higher farmer income. Sales, buyer interest and net returns are tracked separately and reported only when supported by evidence.</p></div></section>

    <section class="section section-white" id="indicators"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="kicker">Geographical & outcome indicators</div><h2>Show where the event reaches—and what changes.</h2><p>Collect only information needed for evaluation. Do not publish personal contact details or precise farm locations without explicit consent.</p></div><div class="metric-grid">
      <div class="metric"><strong>1. Local reach</strong><p>Participant count by village or ward, beginning in Kavutiri and extending to other Embu locations actually represented.</p></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>2. Stakeholder reach</strong><p>Number and type of farmers, cooperatives, businesses, schools, public bodies and technology organizations represented.</p></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>3. Systems tested</strong><p>Number of websites, forms and email workflows tested, with test cases and pass/fail evidence.</p></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>4. Technical improvement</strong><p>Compatibility issues found, corrected or demonstrated, plus outstanding issues with assigned follow-up.</p></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>5. Practical business steps</strong><p>Farmers or businesses identifying a specific next step, such as preparing product information or contacting a cooperative.</p></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>6. Commercial follow-through</strong><p>Buyer enquiries, sample requests, negotiations and completed sales tracked after the event; income effects only where verified.</p></div>
    </div></div></section>

    <section class="section" id="outputs"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="kicker">Concrete outputs</div><h2>What the event will leave behind.</h2></div><div class="grid3">
      <article class="card"><div class="num">A</div><h3>Technical test report</h3><p>Test cases, outcomes, supporting evidence, identified compatibility gaps and retest results.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><div class="num">B</div><h3>Preliminary UA-readiness assessment</h3><p>Observed strengths, gaps, recommendations and issues requiring further technical work.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><div class="num">C</div><h3>Geographic and stakeholder summary</h3><p>Participant reach by location and group, with privacy-conscious reporting.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><div class="num">D</div><h3>Follow-up action plan</h3><p>Prioritized actions, proposed owners and the evidence needed to verify completion.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><div class="num">E</div><h3>Community learning materials</h3><p>Event posters, flyers, T-shirts and coffee packaging with QR codes linking to approved digital resources.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><div class="num">F</div><h3>Post-event report</h3><p>Findings, participant feedback, relevant photographs and lessons learned, submitted within the applicable UA Day reporting deadline.</p></article>
    </div></div></section>

    <section class="section section-white" id="languages"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div class="kicker">Language is part of access</div><h2>One event. Five languages. A shared opportunity.</h2><p>Local-language facilitation allows people to contribute in the language they understand best. Technical demonstrations will use suitable internet-enabled equipment where required; a feature phone alone may not support browsing, QR scanning or email.</p></div><div class="lang-row"><span class="lang">Kiembu</span><span class="lang">Kiswahili</span><span class="lang">English</span><span class="lang">Kimeru</span><span class="lang">Kikuyu</span></div><p class="small">Potential collaborators—including farmer groups, educational institutions, county stakeholders and technology practitioners—will be approached. Their participation is not confirmed unless explicitly agreed.</p></div></section>

    <section class="footer-cta"><div class="wrap footer-grid"><div><div class="eyebrow">Rustic Garden · Kavutiri, Embu County</div><h2>Better digital systems should work for more people.</h2><p>The farmer brings the knowledge and the product. Universal Acceptance provides a technical lens. The event brings people together to test, document and act on evidence.</p><p><strong>Event contact:</strong> <a href="mailto:groupnomad70@gmail.com">groupnomad70@gmail.com</a><br><strong>Rustic Garden:</strong> <a href="https://rustic-garden.github.io/Rustic-Garden/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visit the website ↗</a></p></div><div><a class="btn btn-primary" href="#objective">Return to objective ↑</a></div></div></section>
  </main>
